WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.336
-Our next guest serves as
national political correspondent

00:00:03.336 --> 00:00:04.970
for NBC News and MSNBC.

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:07.974
He also hosts MSNBC's newest
original podcast

00:00:07.974 --> 00:00:09.375
"The Revolution with
Steve Kornacki,"

00:00:09.375 --> 00:00:11.111
which is available now.
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:11.111 --> 00:00:12.879
our friend Steve Kornacki,
everybody!

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:14.614
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:14.614 --> 00:00:21.354 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:28.061 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:28.061 --> 00:00:29.395
I'll just jump in.

00:00:29.395 --> 00:00:31.631
I'll ask the question everyone
would want me to ask.

00:00:31.631 --> 00:00:33.232
How much sleep have you had?

00:00:33.232 --> 00:00:35.234
-80 minutes.
-An 80-minute nap.

00:00:35.234 --> 00:00:37.470
When did the 80-minute nap
take place?

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.240
-About an hour
before I came over here.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.341
If I'm a little groggy,
I apologize.

00:00:42.341 --> 00:00:45.311
-So you know, you have
a reputation, a well-earned one,

00:00:45.311 --> 00:00:47.680
of being able to process this
data as it's coming in

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:51.217
in real time and helping explain
it to those of us

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:53.052
who are laymen watching at home.

00:00:53.052 --> 00:00:55.288
Do you ever worry about how fast
it's going to come in,

00:00:55.288 --> 00:00:57.190
or at this point,
does adrenaline kick in

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:58.458
and you know you can do it?

00:00:58.458 --> 00:01:02.428
-No, no, it's constant fear
and panic.

00:01:02.428 --> 00:01:05.798
It reminds me of studying
for a test or something.

00:01:05.798 --> 00:01:08.701
And it's just like --
As I was telling people

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:10.370
before the election,
I'm the only person in America

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.672
who wishes we had another month
of the campaign

00:01:12.672 --> 00:01:15.374
because I needed the prep time
to get ready for it.

00:01:15.374 --> 00:01:17.944
-Can you put last night --
And again, obviously the grain

00:01:17.944 --> 00:01:20.479
of salt, nothing really has been
decided as far as

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:22.949
who's controlling
the two chambers.

00:01:22.949 --> 00:01:25.318
But historical context,

00:01:25.318 --> 00:01:27.887
this does seem like a pretty
good night for Democrats.

00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:30.223
-I mean, the fact that a day
later we're sitting here saying

00:01:30.223 --> 00:01:31.824
we don't know yet officially

00:01:31.824 --> 00:01:33.159
who's going to
control the House,

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:34.827
we don't know who's going to
control the Senate --

00:01:34.827 --> 00:01:36.262
the Democrats certainly have
a chance,

00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:38.064
a path to controlling
the Senate --

00:01:38.064 --> 00:01:41.034
that in and of itself I think is
just an achievement for them,

00:01:41.034 --> 00:01:44.570
relative to how these midterm
elections have gone in the past,

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:47.072
especially when
the sitting president has

00:01:47.072 --> 00:01:48.908
the approval rating
that Joe Biden has.

00:01:48.908 --> 00:01:50.743
Because you look at the
presidents in the past who have

00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:52.545
had good midterms
and they've had like

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:55.148
65%, 70% approval ratings.

00:01:55.148 --> 00:01:57.015
Joe Biden is sitting at 44%.

00:01:57.015 --> 00:01:59.485
So I think that's the thing that
caught people off guard

00:01:59.485 --> 00:02:03.155
was you look at Biden, you look
at his popularity,

00:02:03.155 --> 00:02:05.158
you look at the attitudes about
the economy,

00:02:05.158 --> 00:02:08.427
you're just expecting his party
to kind of take it on the chin.

00:02:08.427 --> 00:02:10.796
And whatever happens,
they may lose the House still,

00:02:10.796 --> 00:02:13.165
they may end up losing 10, 15
seats or something,

00:02:13.165 --> 00:02:15.601
but that's not historically
taking on it the chin.

00:02:15.601 --> 00:02:19.004
-So it did seem like a
repudiation of Donald Trump.

00:02:19.004 --> 00:02:22.976
Right? He picked a lot of
candidates and the Republicans

00:02:22.976 --> 00:02:24.410
who distanced themselves more
from Trump,

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.679
certainly Ron DeSantis in
Florida, I feel like that's what

00:02:26.679 --> 00:02:28.547
you're going to be seeing Fox
News crowing about

00:02:28.547 --> 00:02:29.716
over the next couple of days.

00:02:29.716 --> 00:02:32.017
They're going move the narrative
over to that.

00:02:32.017 --> 00:02:35.521
Do you think Donald Trump
continues to be sort of

00:02:35.521 --> 00:02:38.157
an anchor to
the Republican Party

00:02:38.157 --> 00:02:40.126
taking back a large majority?

00:02:40.126 --> 00:02:42.095
-I really wonder what's going to
happen now

00:02:42.095 --> 00:02:45.264
because he literally, on
the morning of the election, was

00:02:45.264 --> 00:02:49.068
teasing this idea of
a November 15th announcement

00:02:49.068 --> 00:02:50.703
that he's running again in 2024.

00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:51.904
I'm really curious after this.

00:02:51.904 --> 00:02:53.472
Is he going to follow through
with that now?

00:02:53.472 --> 00:02:54.874
Are there folks around him
telling him,

00:02:54.874 --> 00:02:57.075
"Hey, maybe back off
for a little bit here"?

00:02:57.075 --> 00:03:00.113
And it does seem like there have
been a bunch of moments

00:03:00.113 --> 00:03:02.849
since 2015 when Donald Trump
first emerged

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:07.153
where you kind of wondered is
this the moment when sort of

00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:09.989
the Republican establishment,
whatever you want to call it,

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:12.825
can kind of coalesce
around a different path?

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:15.428
Because a lot of them want to
and have kind of, you know,

00:03:15.428 --> 00:03:17.630
for various reasons have not
done it.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:20.733
And you wonder if DeSantis
winning so decisively

00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:22.835
in Florida, with Trump going
after him,

00:03:22.835 --> 00:03:25.304
now there's clearly separation
between the two of them.

00:03:25.304 --> 00:03:28.674
For that matter in Georgia with
Brian Kemp, the governor there.

00:03:28.674 --> 00:03:31.510
Trump came after him in the
primary earlier this year.

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:33.346
Kemp survived that primary
easily.

00:03:33.346 --> 00:03:35.815
He won last night easily
in Georgia.

00:03:35.815 --> 00:03:40.019
Is there sort of a coalition
that's kind of emerging there

00:03:40.019 --> 00:03:42.387
that could move the Republican
Party in a different direction?

00:03:42.387 --> 00:03:43.556
I don't know, though.

00:03:43.556 --> 00:03:45.625
That question's been raised many
times since 2015.

00:03:45.625 --> 00:03:48.494
The answer's been no every time.

00:03:48.494 --> 00:03:51.497
-Exit polling, is it still
too early to tell

00:03:51.497 --> 00:03:55.167
or do you think we can say that
abortion was a bigger issue

00:03:55.167 --> 00:03:58.371
than maybe in the weeks leading
up to it we thought it would be?

00:03:58.371 --> 00:04:00.306
-Yeah, I'm kind of two minds.

00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:01.741
I think it definitely
could have been.

00:04:01.741 --> 00:04:03.876
I think it definitely could have
motivated a lot of Democrats,

00:04:03.876 --> 00:04:06.579
who as I say, didn't think
Joe Biden --

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:09.015
didn't think the world of
Joe Biden to vote anyway.

00:04:09.015 --> 00:04:11.350
I tend to think
the Trump factor, though,

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:14.253
was big in this thing 'cause
you can look at Republicans

00:04:14.253 --> 00:04:17.023
who had success. You talk about
DeSantis in Florida.

00:04:17.023 --> 00:04:20.426
DeSantis is pro-life,
so it didn't affect him.

00:04:20.426 --> 00:04:23.429
It didn't keep him from doing
incredibly well in Florida.

00:04:23.429 --> 00:04:27.132
I think where you saw the real
separation between Republicans,

00:04:27.132 --> 00:04:28.434
a place like Georgia.

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:30.035
Herschel Walker, Brian Kemp.

00:04:30.035 --> 00:04:31.370
They're both pro-life.

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:33.405
But Herschel Walker is the one
who's underperforming

00:04:33.405 --> 00:04:34.707
considerably here

00:04:34.707 --> 00:04:36.542
and I think the Trump factor has
a lot to do with that.

00:04:36.542 --> 00:04:38.377
-And I guess candidates matter,
right?

00:04:38.377 --> 00:04:41.580
I think that we talk a lot about
how divided the country is

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:43.983
and how people tend to vote
along party lines.

00:04:43.983 --> 00:04:46.251
But certainly, when you put Kemp
against Herschel Walker,

00:04:46.251 --> 00:04:48.487
for example, that's the same
state, and you can tell

00:04:48.487 --> 00:04:50.823
that's really just an issue of
candidates, wouldn't you say?

00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:53.259
-Yeah. Look.

00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:55.861
There's a voter in Georgia,
and in Georgia it's really

00:04:55.861 --> 00:05:00.133
in the Atlanta suburbs, who is
not nuts about Joe Biden,

00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:01.834
really doesn't like
the Democratic Party,

00:05:01.834 --> 00:05:03.736
but really doesn't like
Donald Trump.

00:05:03.736 --> 00:05:07.006
And Kemp checks every box
for them

00:05:07.006 --> 00:05:09.842
because Kemp isn't a Democrat,
he's a conservative,

00:05:09.842 --> 00:05:13.145
but he also vouched for the 2020
election in Georgia.

00:05:13.145 --> 00:05:14.246
Trump said, "Well, that's it.

00:05:14.246 --> 00:05:15.848
That disqualifies you
as governor.

00:05:15.848 --> 00:05:18.151
I'm sending in somebody to run
against you in the primary."

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:20.386
And Kemp stood his ground
and won the primary.

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:21.654
And to that voter,

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:23.689
Kemp suddenly became
a very attractive candidate.

00:05:23.689 --> 00:05:26.992
Somebody like Herschel Walker,
who's much more closely aligned

00:05:26.992 --> 00:05:29.328
with Donald Trump, it doesn't
have that kind of a story.

00:05:29.328 --> 00:05:32.565
You could see these big
Republican counties sort of

00:05:32.565 --> 00:05:34.333
on the outskirts of the Atlanta
metro area,

00:05:34.333 --> 00:05:35.534
it was really striking.

00:05:35.534 --> 00:05:37.269
You're seeing these 7-, 8-point
differences

00:05:37.269 --> 00:05:38.504
between where Kemp is running

00:05:38.504 --> 00:05:39.972
and where Walker is running
last night.

00:05:39.972 --> 00:05:43.810
-How far could I zoom in on
a district without you knowing

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:45.811
what district it was
on one of those maps?

00:05:45.811 --> 00:05:47.680
[ Laughter ]
-Is this the setup
for a test here?

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.249
-No, it's not.

00:05:50.249 --> 00:05:52.551
If I just showed you the lines
of a county,

00:05:52.551 --> 00:05:54.086
you'd be like,
"I know where that is."

00:05:54.086 --> 00:05:56.956
-There's 3,100 of them. Trust
me, a lot of them look similar.

00:05:56.956 --> 00:05:59.058
[ Laughter ]

00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:02.094
-You have this podcast,
"The Revolution."

00:06:02.094 --> 00:06:03.963
This is about Newt Gingrich.
This is about 1994.

00:06:03.963 --> 00:06:07.166
Was this something that has
fascinated you for a long time?

00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:10.869
-Yeah, I mean, '94 was one of
the first elections, you know --

00:06:10.869 --> 00:06:12.905
Believe it or not, I was kind of
into this stuff as a kid.

00:06:12.905 --> 00:06:15.842
[ Laughter ]

00:06:15.842 --> 00:06:17.777
So '94 was kind of
a formative one for me.

00:06:17.777 --> 00:06:21.180
But it was a big deal because I
just remember the Democrats had

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:22.482
controlled Congress,
they controlled the House

00:06:22.482 --> 00:06:25.451
for 40 straight years
heading into '94.

00:06:25.451 --> 00:06:28.254
And I remember my introduction
to politics was that it was just

00:06:28.254 --> 00:06:30.288
the idea of Republicans
controlling Congress

00:06:30.288 --> 00:06:31.624
was just unthinkable.

00:06:31.624 --> 00:06:33.993
In '94, all of a sudden,
it happens.

00:06:33.993 --> 00:06:36.162
And as I look back -- It's
a point of reference for me,

00:06:36.162 --> 00:06:38.931
but as I look back over the last
generation, I really think

00:06:38.931 --> 00:06:42.368
it was the starting point of
where we are today,

00:06:42.368 --> 00:06:44.871
which is politics really is
this nationalized thing.

00:06:44.871 --> 00:06:46.305
I grew up in Massachusetts.

00:06:46.305 --> 00:06:48.974
Tip O'Neill, when I was a kid,
was a towering political figure.

00:06:48.974 --> 00:06:50.676
He was the Speaker of the House
in the 1980s.

00:06:50.676 --> 00:06:53.445
He had this saying,
"All politics is local."

00:06:53.445 --> 00:06:55.247
And everyone would just repeat
that as if it was --

00:06:55.247 --> 00:06:58.250
This was the high understanding
in politics.

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:00.819
It's not anymore.
It's almost all national now.

00:07:00.819 --> 00:07:03.155
People, Democrat, Republican,
red, blue,

00:07:03.155 --> 00:07:06.058
they follow national stories,
national characters,

00:07:06.058 --> 00:07:09.862
they're motivated by national
issues, national storylines.

00:07:09.862 --> 00:07:14.199
I think '94 was really the first
nationalized midterm election.

00:07:14.199 --> 00:07:16.468
And it's really kind of grown
into what it is today.

00:07:16.468 --> 00:07:17.870
-You mentioned
you were interested

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:19.671
in this at a young age.

00:07:19.671 --> 00:07:22.107
By the way, everybody laughed.
I think they believe you.

00:07:22.107 --> 00:07:23.643
But --
[ Laughter ]

00:07:23.643 --> 00:07:26.812
I also want to point out, you
have brought video evidence.

00:07:26.812 --> 00:07:29.949
This is local-access television?

00:07:29.949 --> 00:07:31.884
-Somebody dug this up. Yeah.

00:07:31.884 --> 00:07:34.453
My high school in Massachusetts,
there were two kids.

00:07:34.453 --> 00:07:36.689
They hosted a comedy show.
-Yeah.

00:07:36.689 --> 00:07:37.956
-On the public-access channel,

00:07:37.956 --> 00:07:39.625
which was in the basement of
our high school.

00:07:39.625 --> 00:07:43.229
And they asked me to sit down
and just do commentary on --

00:07:43.229 --> 00:07:45.264
I think it was the '94 election.

00:07:45.264 --> 00:07:48.333
And I realized when they aired
it, they thought --

00:07:48.333 --> 00:07:49.768
I was playing it straight.

00:07:49.768 --> 00:07:51.904
But they figured it was so
boring, it would be funny.

00:07:51.904 --> 00:07:55.407
[ Laughter ]
-I should note --

00:07:55.407 --> 00:07:57.576
When you go back and you watch
people from high school,

00:07:57.576 --> 00:07:59.645
it's always fascinating to see
how they turned out.

00:07:59.645 --> 00:08:02.882
You have been the straightest
line of anybody I've ever seen

00:08:02.882 --> 00:08:05.051
from who you were last night
to who you are here.

00:08:05.051 --> 00:08:06.185
Take a look.

00:08:08.988 --> 00:08:11.590
-As mandated by
the Constitution, 33 seats

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:15.228
or one-third of the Senate will
be up for re-election in 1994.

00:08:16.662 --> 00:08:20.266
Since it would be impractical
for me to go over each race,

00:08:20.266 --> 00:08:22.668
I'll only go over a few of
the highlights this year.

00:08:22.668 --> 00:08:26.138
The Senate balance is 56-44 for
the Democrats as of this moment.

00:08:26.138 --> 00:08:29.575
It is highly plausible that
the Republicans could take over

00:08:29.575 --> 00:08:32.244
the Senate for the first time
since 1986.

00:08:32.244 --> 00:08:34.279
That's all for now.
There's other races.

00:08:34.279 --> 00:08:36.983
Perhaps another time,
I'll talk about more.

00:08:36.983 --> 00:08:41.753
[ Laughter ]
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:41.753 --> 00:08:44.456
-I just wish -- I wish it ended
with like, "And one day,

00:08:44.456 --> 00:08:46.024
I'm going to have a giant map

00:08:46.024 --> 00:08:48.860
and I'm going to be able to
zoom in on that map."

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:50.663
Hey, it was really --

00:08:50.663 --> 00:08:53.032
I mean this from the bottom of
my heart.

00:08:53.032 --> 00:08:54.333
Every time I go through this,

00:08:54.333 --> 00:08:55.968
it's really lovely to have you
sort of leading the way.

00:08:55.968 --> 00:08:57.103
Thanks so much for making time
for us

00:08:57.103 --> 00:08:58.603
when you've had 80 minutes of
sleep.

00:08:58.603 --> 00:09:00.105
[ Cheers and applause ]
Really appreciate it.

00:09:00.105 --> 00:09:01.707
Steve Kornacki, everybody.

00:09:01.707 --> 00:09:05.411
All six episodes of his podcast
"The Revolution" available now.

00:09:05.411 --> 00:09:07.013
We'll be right back with more
"Late Night."

